    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 171139\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n439 AM PDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Light rain is possible Saturday afternoon into Sunday, but rain \n  impacts are not likely through the weekend. \n\n* Precipitation chances and winds return to the region early to\n  middle of next week, with significant uncertainty in forecast\n  and impact details.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Friday\n\nRadar and satellite imagery through the night showed a decently \norganized circulation over southeast California/southwest Arizona\nas rain and lightning developed. This is likely associated with \na piece of energy that was cut off and left behind from the \nshortwave the past few days. Hi-res models have been slow to pick \nup this feature and what it will do the next 36 hours. Surface \nanalysis showed around 1.00 PWATs with this feature that were \nslowly creeping north. HREF has a decent handle on this and shows \nPWATs increase to over 0.75 inches by Sunday morning through the \nColorado River Valley and western Arizona. Modest moisture \ncombined with vorticity advection rotating around the center of \nthe low should be enough to light rain to develop at times late \nSaturday afternoon into Sunday. Unsure how much will reach the \nground, and probabilities for impactful rainfall amounts is low- \nso likely will not see rainfall impacts with this round of \nprecipitation. If better rainfall were to develop, it would be in \nthe terrain of Mohave and Clark counties as easterly upslope flow \ncould enhance light rain. 90th percentile \"worst case scenario\" \n24hr rainfall amounts show about 0.50 inches of rain in these \nareas, so high impact weather is not expected. Given the models \nseem behind on picking up on this piece of energy and what it will\ndo, mesoanalysis late morning and early afternoon will paint a \nbetter picture of what may occur and adjustments to precipitation \nchances are possible. Otherwise, winds should remain light as \ntemperatures drop to slightly below normal. \n\nAfter this first system lifts out and is absorbed into the mean\nflow, another more potent trough is expected to impact the \nregion Tuesday through much of the work week. On Tuesday as the \ndeepening trough approaches, breezy south to southwest winds \ndevelop. Probabilities for impactful wind gusts over 40 MPH have \ndropped and generally remain below 50%, so wind impacts are \nbecoming less of a concern. With strong forcing associated with \nthe cutoff low and moisture in place over the region, \nprecipitation chances are possible starting Tuesday and continuing\nta times through the week. Current forecast has chances peaking \nWednesday into Thursday with all areas seeing at least a 30% \nchance for precipitation. Highest chances and precipitation \namounts will be in Inyo County through the Southern Great Basin \nwhere the strongest forcing is currently expected to set up. \nAccumulating snow is also likely, mainly above 9000ft. All that \nsaid- there continues to be significant uncertainty in the track \nand strength of the system as it moves inland, as well as \ndiscrepancies in how much moisture will be available. Increasing \nconfidence that there will be precipitation next week, especially \nWednesday and Thursday, but continued uncertainty in exact details\nand impacts as model disagreement persists. With increasing cloud\ncover and precipitation chances, temperatures will remain below \nnormal next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nnortheasterly winds under 10KT will continue through sunset this \nevening, when winds veer to the southwest and then become variable\novernight. Clouds gradually increase in coverage through the \nforecast period, with ceilings becoming SCT-BKN between 7-10kft \nthis evening and tonight as showers drift northward into the area.\nConfidence in impacts at the terminal aside from light rain or \nsprinkles remains low. Until then, VFR conditions will continue.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will generally\nremain around 5-10KT the next 24 hours, maintaining a northerly \nto northeasterly direction for most terminals before settling into\ntypical directional patterns with some variability this evening. \nExceptions include KDAG, where winds remain easterly through the \nperiod, with gusts to around 20-25KT expected late morning through\nlate afternoon, and KIFP where northerly winds will remain \nelevated around 10-15KT this morning and afternoon. Mid-level and \nhigh clouds will increase in coverage from south to north today, \nwith ceilings dropping to around 6-10kft this evening and tonight \nas isolated to scattered showers overspread southeastern \nCalifornia, southern Nevada, and northwestern Arizona.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
    "risk": "MEDIUM",
